\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath}

\title{Pressure Drop Estimates for a Retrofitted Cooling-Water Loop}
\author{Process Engineering Group, North Plant}
\date{June 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
The north plant cooling-water loop was commissioned in 1998 and has
been retrofitted twice, most recently in 2024 when two parallel plate
heat exchangers replaced the original shell-and-tube units. Operators
report that the loop now runs closer to its pressure limit than the
design documents suggest, and the present note re-estimates the
frictional pressure drop with measured roughness values.

Flow in the main header is turbulent throughout the operating
envelope. The Reynolds number is evaluated from
\begin{equation}
Re = \frac{\rho u D}{\mu}
\end{equation}
with properties taken at the bulk temperature. Measured values during
the May campaign span $Re = 4.1\times 10^{4}$ to $9.6\times 10^{4}$,
comfortably above the transition region.

\section{Model}
The frictional pressure drop follows the Darcy--Weisbach relation
\begin{equation}
\Delta p = f \frac{L}{D} \frac{\rho u^2}{2}
\end{equation}
where $f$ is the Darcy friction factor, $L$ the equivalent length
including fittings, and $D$ the header diameter. Equivalent lengths
for the retrofit were taken from the 2024 as-built drawings, with the
coefficient method used for bends and the full-bore value for the port
losses of the new plate exchangers.

Surface roughness was measured on four spool pieces removed during
the 2025 outage. The values cluster around $0.12$ mm, well above the
drawn-tube figure assumed in the original design basis.

% TODO: Blasius screening correlation to be added here (group agreed 2026-06).

\section{Field Data}
Superficial velocity is obtained from the metered volumetric flow as
\begin{gather}
u = \frac{Q}{A}
\end{gather}
using the inside cross-section of the main header. Flow readings were
logged at one-minute intervals over the campaign and averaged per
shift; the shift averages are the basis for all estimates reported
here.

The measured header pressure drop at the metered flows sits ten to
fifteen percent above the value predicted with the original roughness
assumption, which motivated the remeasurement described above.

\section{Conclusion}
Revised estimates with measured roughness close most of the gap
between predicted and observed pressure drop. The remaining
discrepancies lie within meter uncertainty and do not justify further
derating of the loop.
\end{document}

